WEBVTT

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:45.680
SPEAKS POLISH This is Gdansk.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:51.080
You can count on anywhere in the northern Baltic having at least two names.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.480
Two languages.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.000
Two cultures.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.840
Two former colonisers, at least.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.840
Gdansk is, currently, in Poland.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:09.720
Throughout its history it has been occupied and reoccupied, subjugated and liberated.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.200
Allotment gardens did not belong to hobbyists.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.240
They were near necessities throughout long decades of shortages.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:30.400
They are de facto monuments to oppression and hunger in a part of Europe which abounds in official monuments, mostly monuments to hypocrisy.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:40.160
Gdansk might be said to be the founding city of modern Poland.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:54.600
The shipyard strikes initially occasioned by the sacking of the crane operator and former welder Anna Walentynowicz were led by Lech Walesa, and led in turn to the founding of Solidarity.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.920
Which in further turn, led to suppression, martial law and state-sanctioned murder.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.880
This is where the Soviet Empire first began to crumble.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:16.560
Where Communism, toothless and weakened by itself, began to capitulate in a way that it hadn't during the Hungarian uprising or the Prague Spring.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:28.160
This might be considered appropriate, but Gdansk belonged to the Hanseatic League, God's first attempt at the EU, which was born out of something other than an "ism".

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.240
It grew rich out of enterprise, out of trade.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:40.440
Of course trade may also be an ideology, but it's an ideology with a small "i" and it's practical, flexible, undogmatic.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:49.560
W B Yeats wrote: "The best lack all conviction, "the worst are full of passionate intensity."

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:56.480
It's unlikely that he had in mind the bourgeois merchants of the Hanse as exemplars of the best.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.200
The Hanse were civilian.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.400
Their virtues were civic.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.440
They made cities, not war.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.960
They did not raise armies.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.760
Nor did they put to sail in men of war.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:38.960
Nonetheless, Hanse shipping required protection against pirates and privateers such as the Victuals Brotherhood, who were based on the island of Gotland.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.840
Which may or may not be the original home of the Goths.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.760
And Hanse cities too needed defences.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:07.560
Security solutions were outsourced - "ausgliedern" in German.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:22.320
The mercenary brawn with whom the Hanse entered into an enduring contract was the Order of Teutonic Knights, a sect of muscular Christian warriors formed in Palestine during the Crusades.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:30.600
They had a taste for tournaments and their buildings are tournament pavilions frozen forever in brick.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:45.360
The Teutonic Knights were supposedly celibate, supposedly chivalric, certainly militant, given to hunting and practised in faith-based belligerence initiatives.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:52.240
They were hungry for territory, which they received in return for their freelance campaigns.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:58.040
Missionary zeal is invariably a beard for colonisation.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:03.640
The Teutonic Knights and the Hanse became mutually dependent.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.360
The Order built castles.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:11.280
Castles such as Marienburg, now Malbork, in the hinterland of Gdansk.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.880
Its sheer immensity had not previously been seen.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:19.840
It remains startling today, a red brick Carcassonne.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:25.400
Largely unknown in Britain because, unlike Carcassonne, it belongs to the north.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:48.320
The Teutonic Knights considered conversion by force to be legitimate, even if the non-believers in question, that is, non-Christians, posed neither the ideological nor the political threat that Islam did in the countries bordering the eastern Mediterranean.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:06:08.280
It was considered especially legitimate if the unfortunate non-believers occupied lands that were rich in minerals and natural products - trees, furry animals, fossilised resins such as amber, aurochs, tar, peat.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:31.880
The Order's crusade or land grab was directed against Lithuania, the last pagan country, sandwiched between the easternmost dioceses of the Roman church and the westernmost outposts of the Russian Orthodox.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:49.720
It's largely due to these churches' black propaganda that we've been brainwashed into believing that paganism signifies incivility, coarseness, violence and so on.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.400
It didn't and it still doesn't.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:58.840
Despite its having been co-opted by various guises of Heavy Metal.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:30.800
Pagan was a blanket word, born of fanatical zeal and bigoted incuriosity, which dismissed countless different sorts of belief.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:37.120
It's a synonym of heathen and it derives ultimately from the same root as the word "peasant".

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:41.600
It signifies, then, people who lived in the country.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:46.880
That's to say, in the forests rather than in towns.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:57.760
During the middle ages, Lithuania was the most organised, most powerful state in eastern Europe.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:00.240
It was officially pagan.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:06.560
It was however religiously tolerant and did not persecute Christians or Jews.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.520
Its people mostly venerated nature.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.000
They were animists.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:14.680
That's to say they regarded everything as possessing some sort of spirit or soul.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.760
Something along those lines anyway.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.520
They were polytheistic.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:37.000
Their pantheon included a god of the sun, a god of the moon, a god of lightning, a god of rain, a god of drizzle, a god of possible precipitation by the end of the eleventh day after the first apple ripens.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.560
Flowers, fruit, crops, trees...

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:53.480
These were all worshipped, especially trees.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:57.720
Humans were reincarnated as trees.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:00.400
Trees formed sacred groves.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:13.400
The forest, as dark a place to medieval Christians as it was to the thinkers of the Enlightenment, was to these pagans the very site of their civilisation.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:23.960
People ritually dressed as storks, bears, bulls, stags, goats, horses and boars.

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:35.040
Their religion or system of superstition was indigenous and as appropriate as religion ever could be since it revered the materials that their life depended on.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:40.480
Wood, water, horses, berries, fungus and so on.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.960
And it thanked the imaginary gods for them.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:49.520
The ubiquitous forests are full of beasts.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:54.520
They're dark, enclosing, monotonous, frightening.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:10:00.520
The bark of birches compensates for the lack of venomous snakes.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:05.920
Grass snakes were worshipped for want of other snakes.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:12.760
Deserts incite a different sort of fantasy or superstition.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.000
Fantasy of monotheism.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:28.200
At least that's what it seems for the three Abrahamic monotheistic cults, Judaism, Christianity and Islam, all emerged from the same Middle Eastern deserts and believe in the same god.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:41.000
The fun and games down the years has preposterously been about which particular prophet owns the exclusive rights to the God franchise.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:11:00.200
And so these monotheistic faiths might be considered singularly unsuitable in the cold, dank north of birches, pines, mosquitoes, bogs and bog bodies.

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.560
However, Christianity did thrive.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:19.720
The Teutonic Knights knocked the love of one god, one fictive delusion, into people who had previously had many gods, many fictive delusions.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:31.440
The Knights' evangelical assault suggests that a religion is no more than a cult with an army attached to it.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:42.440
The indigenous religion was driven underground, to be periodically exhumed by those with elephantine folk-memories.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:57.080
The imported religion promised that no matter how dour this life may be, and life in the north was dour, everything will be just dandy in the next life if you believe in and worship the one god.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:00.800
So it enjoyed an understandable appeal.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:15.920
The number of churches in Vilnius might be held to attest to that appeal.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.440
The city is as triumphantly baroque as Rome.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:33.480
The Counter-Reformation style of the Catholic church was gross, vulgar, populist, energetic, thrilling.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.200
But it was hardly Lithuanian.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:44.840
It is Italian, by way of Poland, which had annexed Lithuania.

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:49.400
So the majority of these churches were designed by Poles.

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:58.400
And like most architects before and since, they were enthusiastic followers of fashion.

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:05.160
They doggedly ransacked the Italian pattern book for ideas.

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:14.280
This was then a double colonisation, a double invasion.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:16.960
But that is Lithuania's fate.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:28.520
It has been frequently invaded and colonised and annexed that its buildings are the calling cards of its violators, who, of course, did not see themselves as violators.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:33.880
These churches may imitate Italy's, but they could never be taken for Italian.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:41.920
Their surroundings, their conjunction with the street, their materials, the light that illuminates them...

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:46.040
All these are unmistakably northern.

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:56.440
And so is the routinely vain attempt to appear southern, to seize at a link with the sun if only by association.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:08.840
Vilnius's most fascinating church however is an exception to the touchingly hopeless aspiration to southerness.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:11.360
Furthermore it is not baroque.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:14.600
Well, not formally baroque, not chronologically baroque.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:19.360
It belongs actually to the later Middle Ages.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:30.880
Yet in the restlessness of its many devices and in the very form of certain of those devices, it appears to predict the baroque.

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:40.160
Because architectural history is founded in what happened in the south, in Italy, it remains unthinkingly biased towards that country.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:48.560
So to talk of an architectural renaissance in the north is to apply to northern Europe the inappropriate model of the south.

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:51.960
In the north, things happened differently.

00:14:51.440 --> 00:15:11.000
The gothic, born in northern France and naturalised everywhere north of northern France, endured far longer here than it did in places south of the Loire - places such as Italy, where gothic was a term of abuse, like pagan.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:29.000
More than half a millennium after it was supposedly Christianised, Lithuania still clings both to vestiges of its pagan polytheism and to a preoccupation with its forests.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:39.440
One reason for this is because forests are larders - full of partridges, boars, bears... ..and even beaver.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:44.360
I've never eaten bear but I have eaten beaver.

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:46.160
And I highly recommend it.

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:53.280
Oh Christ...

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:56.960
Not this kind of beaver though.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:16:01.560
This beaver is... like spaniel.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:07.160
It's like spaniel dipped in cod liver oil.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:19.440
Lithuania's lakes, too, are larders.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:26.240
Perch, roach, tench, chubb, and most especially pike and carp.

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:33.200
They're caught for the table, not to be put in a keep net, photographed and thrown back.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:43.520
The Jewish, specifically Ashkenazi culinary repertoire includes countless recipes for freshwater fish.

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:48.160
In The Book of Jewish Food...

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.600
Well named, for it is THE book...

00:16:49.120 --> 00:17:02.200
Claudia Roden lists sweet and sour, jellied, formed into dumplings, fried and eaten cold, salted, fried and subsequently pickled.

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:08.079
Before they murdered their victims, the Nazis devised many minor humiliations for them.

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:14.400
Leviticus, for instance, specifically prohibits the tattooing of the human body.

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:32.000
Again, Auschwitz was revered and renowned throughout the Jewish world for the quality of the carp that had been bred in ponds there since they were introduced by Jewish merchants from China in the 1600s.

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:38.560
Jewish cooking has all but disappeared from Lithuania.

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:40.440
A way of life was extinguished with a race.

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:43.200
What does remain is a curious anomaly.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:54.800
The Karaim in Lithuania are of Crimean and eastern Turkish origin.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:18:03.000
Because they do not acknowledge rabbinical interpretations of the Talmud, they were considered apostates.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:15.800
For this reason and because of their Tartar bloodline, the SS's Race and Settlement Office decreed that the Karaim were not to be considered Jewish.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.160
Thus they were spared.

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:21.680
Some even fought for the Nazis.

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:27.040
In the town of Trakai, a small community still prospers.

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:37.200
It worships at kenessas and runs a series of cafes which specialise in kibinai, which is what Cornish pasties ought to be but seldom are.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:46.880
The dish and word kibinai are evidently related to kibbeh, which are found in the eastern Mediterranean.

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:52.880
This is not just food, this is Karaim food.

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:02.600
The Karaim's cafes stare out onto a medieval castle.

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:11.280
Admittedly, alongside the Teutonic Knights' Malbork, it is little more than a starter castle.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:17.600
Nonetheless it's a graphic illustration of Lithuania's former might.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:33.000
Karaim apart, less than 20,000 Jews survived the Second World War in Lithuania.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:40.120
Probably a quarter of a million had been massacred, in the camps, by firing squads, by mobile gas chambers.

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:47.080
Forests can be made as beautiful or as ugly as humans want them to be.

00:19:53.960 --> 00:20:01.760
Some were of families who had lived here for generations.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:04.280
Some had sought refuge here.

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:16.360
Others had been transported from across Europe to what were to become the earliest killing fields in the war on unarmed civilians.

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:26.600
Many of the murderers however were not Germans but Lithuanians, who considered themselves to be partisans and enthusiastically collaborated with the Nazis.

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:33.760
To them the Nazis, who drove out the occupying Soviet army, were liberators.

00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:46.000
Here perhaps is the defining tragedy of the Baltic States, the way that their people have been forced to make the most terrible compromising choices between murderous regimes.

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:54.680
The Soviet occupations, which totalled more than half a century, cost more lives than the Nazis.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:58.440
Soviet memorials are, then, merely victors' memorials.

00:20:57.520 --> 00:21:06.080
They smugly and mendaciously obliterate the USSR's crimes whilst quite properly drawing attention to those of the Third Reich.

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:08.840
They are of course official memorials.

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:11.680
Yesterday's official memorials.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:39.240
The Hill of Crosses near Siauliai in the north of the country is, on the other hand, entirely unofficial, home-made, extemporised, a site of collective piety and melancholic remembrance - a national shrine.

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:45.680
This is thought to have been a place where pagan sacrifices were made.

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:56.160
It is certainly a place where crosses were erected as early as 1831, at the time of an uprising against Russian rule.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:22:08.240
Whilst its imagery is explicitly Christian it is evidently a vestige of the more ancient faith, which survived, hidden underground during the long years of totalitarian suppression.

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:18.480
During those years, folk art was the faith's only means of expression.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:22.320
That art had a vital purpose as a solace.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:33.480
It was a covert way of being Lithuanian, of taking pride in a nation which might, one day, be restored to its people.

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:42.320
The purpose of the Hill of Crosses quite overcomes the roughness with which it is put together.

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:50.840
It is no doubt somewhat artless, like the dead flowers at the site of a fatal road accident.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:58.600
But it does show that the cliched manifestations of faith and grief and pathos and sadness and...

00:22:58.040 --> 00:22:59.160
Who knows?

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:04.320
Gratitude are more important to a certain mindset than are any aesthetic considerations.

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:12.040
The Hill of Crosses turns pretty much an entire nation into outsider artists.

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:18.560
It's a striking site of impassioned and meaningful inarticulacy.

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:30.160
The naive, untutored work of outsider artists is nothing if not a cry for help, a desperate inchoate plea to an unlistening, uncomprehending cruel world.

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:42.960
And that still applies even if the art is bought at a stall catering to presumably reverent tourists.

00:23:42.360 --> 00:24:02.280
Unlike, say, Lourdes, which is a temple to gullibility and mass-produced kitsch, the Hill of Crosses has the capacity to move because it really is an unselfconscious national shrine born out of suffering and made by the people who suffered.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:07.720
Inevitably the Soviets bulldozed the Hill of Crosses on several occasions.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:13.440
They burnt and smelted the crosses and icons and spread sewage on the site.

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:19.640
Equally inevitably, like some stubborn plant, it reasserted itself.

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:30.040
It was tit for tat.

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:45.880
In its long decline after Khrushchev's deposal in 1964, the Soviet Union, never dreaming that the game might one day be up, persisted in imposing its architectural will on its colonies.

00:24:50.600 --> 00:25:05.320
This former hydrotherapy centre at Druskininkai offered the rain mischiefs, the salty moments, the embrace of Neptune, the healthing shower.

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:22.960
This was a Mecca of re-educative rectal tampons, sulphated mud wallows, peat packs, ideologically improving freezing hoses, climatological cures such as air therapy, going for a walk and solar therapy - sunbathing.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:33.800
There were 10,000 such spa establishments across the Soviet Union, "treating," if that's the word, 60 million people per annum.

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:40.240
Hypertension, psoriasis, endocrine imbalance, lymphatic and renal disorders, the kneels...

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:44.280
There was nothing that these places couldn't cure.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:59.440
It is conceivable that they were merely placebos in concrete and stone, places which institutionalised the folk cures and nature remedies which a gullible public was attached to.

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:05.200
Not least as tokens of an old, superstitious irrationalism.

00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:10.160
They now offer new remedies to an equally gullible public.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:14.360
Remedies such as piscine epidermal therapy.

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:20.600
That's fish eating your dead skin cells.

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:29.560
Like a cat's tongue.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:38.400
There is a lot going on here.

00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:50.760
Organic imagery off the Art Nouveau shelf, an attempt to create an architecture that's both gaudily bombastic and recognisably futuristic.

00:26:52.600 --> 00:27:09.920
These exercises in the "Leonid Brezhnev Plays Las Vegas" style, which is also known as the Dogs In Space Manor, possess the same menacing frivolity as Stalin's distended fantasies of the '40s and early '50s.

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:29.640
At another spa, Marianske Lazne - Marienbad - in the not yet former Czechoslovakia in the last days of Soviet dominion, the medical director, a tiny man with a mad boffin hairdo and mad boffin spectacles, told me with a worrying giggle, "People getting rid negative effect of everyday life.

00:27:28.680 --> 00:27:32.200
"We re-programming them.

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:36.440
"Yes, we re-programme people."

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:43.120
Today the negative effects of everyday life are excised by other means.

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:59.040
Nowadays hotels and clubs in improbably rural, improbably sylvan settings offer fauna and massage.

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:09.560
Communist sexual puritanism, public sexual puritanism anyway, has abated.

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:13.680
Spa patients are now punters.

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:23.600
Steroid-enhanced psychiatric nurses who could toss a caber are now Botox-enhanced artistes who can no doubt also toss a caber.

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:32.320
Every country in the Baltic has its own distinctive form of traditional dancing.

00:28:31.760 --> 00:28:35.360
Poland has pole dancing.

00:28:34.520 --> 00:28:37.600
Of which Dirty Gdansking is a most unusual kind.

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:41.240
MUSIC CHANGES Lapland has Lap dancing.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:45.440
And Lithuania has Lith dancing.

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:49.120
As for Estonia...

00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:55.720
"Estonia nation special has full-on one-to-one.

00:28:54.880 --> 00:29:02.120
"You wanting good old-time experience extra-care service, Mr International Businessman?"

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:03.280
That's something to look forward to.

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:39.360
MAN ON Be meeting at international airport in deluxe luxury with lovely skillworker qualification girls, dressed for showing the mister top international businessman best national secret delights at medieval city of Tallinn.

00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:51.080
Of the Baltic states' capital cities, Tallinn is the most captivating, the smallest, the most northerly and the most easterly.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:52.440
Thus the closest to Russia.

00:29:51.520 --> 00:29:57.800
Yet of these cities it feels by some measure the furthest from the Soviet Union.

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:10.880
Indeed it's hard to believe that this Hanseatic city is a former satellite.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:21.120
It has more successfully extinguished traces of Soviet occupation than its neighbours, Riga and Vilnius.

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:24.840
This is not a risk-free position to adopt.

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:46.480
When in the spring of 2007 it was decided to shift - not destroy, merely move from this position in front of the National Library to a less conspicuous site - a monument to what were laughingly known as Soviet freedom fighters, Estonia was deemed by Moscow to have insulted the sacred feelings of the Russian people.

00:30:45.560 --> 00:31:12.320
Needless to say the protests against the dismantling of the monument were the work of agent provocateurs from the Putinjugend, the freelance paramilitaries happily known as the Nashi.

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:22.760
Putin himself, who bears a personal grudge against Estonia on account of his father's treatment here during the Second World War, reacted with predictably hypocritical fury.

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:25.080
You can take the man out of the KGB.

00:31:30.320 --> 00:31:41.920
The Russian Federation threatened unspecified retaliation, which came in the form of cyber attacks on businesses and institutions.

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:45.520
This aggression was not taken particularly seriously by the self-hating Western liberal media.

00:31:44.680 --> 00:31:49.840
That is to say, most of the Western media.

00:31:51.240 --> 00:32:04.960
Compare that same media's reaction when in 2006 Neo-Nazis polled a whopping six per cent of the vote in the eastern German province of Mecklenburg, West Pomerania.

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:10.280
It was as though the 117-year-old Adolf Hitler himself had returned from his Paraguayan estancia to reclaim power.

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:39.080
The point of course is that despite nearly half a century of cold war, despite the enormity and extent of its crimes, despite the totalitarian nature of Putin's regime, there still exists in Western Europe a risible tolerance of Communism and neo-Communism.

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:45.760
"Old commie" and "Nazi sympathiser" carry entirely different connotations.

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:50.800
The one a lovable class warrior, all Marx and cardies.

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:56.160
The other, an embittered, inadequate foaming paranoiac.

00:32:55.360 --> 00:33:00.920
In fact the same obloquy should be visited on both.

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:01.880
They're both beyond the pale.

00:33:00.960 --> 00:33:07.200
The ghosts of the recent past hang over the Baltic like a toxic cloud.

00:33:06.800 --> 00:33:15.280
When this museum was opened in 2003, it was pointedly called the Museum of Occupations...

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:16.560
Plural.

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:22.760
The short Nazi occupation and the long Soviet one.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:31.160
Russia acted with its usual indignant hypocrisy, claiming that the exhibition puts the USSR on a par with Nazi Germany.

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:34.360
Which was surely the intention.

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:46.520
In much of the former Eastern bloc, in the former satellite states, in the former Soviet Union itself, you are constantly forced to wonder...

00:33:45.720 --> 00:33:51.320
What is it precisely that is so "former" about them?

00:33:57.960 --> 00:34:09.000
The fearfully cautious mentality, the xenophobia and insularity, the longing for regulation and state dependency, the ostalgia...

00:34:08.120 --> 00:34:19.000
These are just some of the signs that, although structures may have changed, minds, conditioned over decades, remain set in concrete.

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:26.040
The only consolation can be that someone usually got the aggregate wrong and that that the concrete crumbles.

00:34:29.639 --> 00:34:33.320
None of this is seems to be the case in Tallinn.

00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:34.960
Tallinn is thriving.

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:37.199
It is also elegant.

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:45.360
It owes much of that elegance to Estonia having been both independent and prosperous between the First and Second World Wars.

00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:51.080
Lithuania was a Polish serf nation and economically devastated.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:55.360
Latvia was independent and economically devastated.

00:34:54.560 --> 00:35:02.840
Estonia linked itself to Finland, to Scandinavia, to Britain by trade.

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:16.720
Architecturally it was alone among the Baltic States in adopting Modernism.

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.640
Rather, modernisms.

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:40.840
One sort of Modernism it went in for suggests that Estonia, with less than one per cent of the population of the Soviet Union, was daring to define itself as the antithesis of its vast neighbour whose own architecture increasingly suggested that Communism was nothing more than age-old Russian nationalism.

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:45.960
This sort of Modernism is northern European.

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:49.320
It's of specifically Hanseatic origin.

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:56.240
We've seen this restless brickwork in the now distant cities of Bremen and Hamburg.

00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:08.920
The second form of modernism Estonia embraced was white, rectilinear, cubistic.

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:18.400
The bourgeoisie of the newly independent state after WWI was massively enthused by it.

00:36:17.520 --> 00:36:24.720
Thousands of houses were built with state loans to encourage property ownership.

00:36:23.840 --> 00:36:30.800
Each house was designed by an architect, the antithesis of British speculative building of the same period.

00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:35.880
The resultant suburbs are as beguiling as those of Brussels.

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:46.720
Modernism's attraction may not have been so much that it presaged the future, which it did, but that it carried no baggage.

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:50.200
It was as far as could be from the architecture imposed by the former coloniser.

00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:59.240
It wasn't, save in the brains of such begetters as Le Corbusier, even European.

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:05.800
Le Corbusier created something new by reproducing something old from a previously ignored culture.

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:19.680
So, through a few influential and persuasive conduits, the architecture of Colomb-Bechar and Casablanca, Tunis and Taroudant, was reproduced on the Baltic shore.

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:24.920
This is what is known as "looking south."

00:37:30.320 --> 00:37:49.280
There can be no other small country, and the entire population is hardly more than that of greater Glasgow, with such an appetite for architecture, especially domestic architecture, and with such an abundance of accomplished architects to satisfy that appetite.

00:38:00.400 --> 00:38:07.680
But there's seldom anything specifically Estonian about the work of architects such as Emil Urbel.

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:19.880
Unless, that is, specifically Estonian in this context means well made, highly crafted and longing to be somewhere that it isn't.

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:28.320
It might be said that this is the elemental purpose of architecture, to create somewhere else.

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:31.880
To create a new common identity.

00:38:33.800 --> 00:38:45.240
In the later 19th century, localisation, folk music, national traditions and national consciousness were an international craze all over Europe.

00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:53.640
In Estonia, rock sport, banned by Peter the Great in 1714, was revived.

00:38:53.280 --> 00:39:09.200
And so was cake-dressing of the dead, the custom whereby the eldest child smears a dead parent's face with batter to ensure that the parent will not go hungry in the journey to the afterlife.

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:16.640
You saying us five Estonian poets you are most studying.

00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:25.680
There holding place lecture at sliding show in international renown Dr Jaan Tamm.

00:39:24.720 --> 00:39:33.040
Rejoice national fish herring from special dancing.

00:39:38.080 --> 00:39:44.920
Special Estonian dancing is no longer national fish herring dancing.

00:40:02.520 --> 00:40:10.240
The dancing that Tallinn now offers the world is Est dancing.

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:16.760
Every year, thousands of hideously dressed, young or youngish ambassadors of British manhood visit Tallinn to enjoy...

00:40:15.960 --> 00:40:17.120
An approximate word...

00:40:16.160 --> 00:40:21.320
What are called stagger nights.

00:40:20.440 --> 00:40:33.680
To get legless, get lurching, get loud, get laid, should they be capable, and get a sexually transmitted disease to take home to Blighty.

00:40:32.920 --> 00:40:43.360
These free-spending lotharios make an impressive contribution to the country's annual billion euro tourist income.

00:40:42.760 --> 00:40:45.680
So they're tolerated.

00:40:44.720 --> 00:40:47.240
Of course they are.

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:51.480
So are their paid dates, mostly itinerant operatives from Russia.

00:40:50.680 --> 00:40:54.240
And their paid dates' kindly guardians.

00:41:00.680 --> 00:41:10.920
This is a city which has been in a hurry to ostentatiously remake itself as part of free-market Europe.

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:18.000
The extent and sheer volume of Tallinn's new commercial building projects - the outward signs of this headlong rush.

00:41:17.080 --> 00:41:20.880
There is a planning authority.

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:27.360
Its Four As-policy - assent, agree, accept, affirm...

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:32.800
Is to say yes... to anything.

00:41:32.920 --> 00:41:51.880
The triumph of laissez-faire and the subjugation of dirigism are creating a city which, unlike the splendid old town and the meticulously disposed bourgeois suburbs, is out of control, a kind of developers' Klondike, the Wild East.

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:54.680
The fact that certain individual buildings are distinguished is not the point.

00:41:53.920 --> 00:42:02.400
It's the cumulative free for all that is so disheartening, so similar to so much of Britain.

00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:03.720
Incoherence rules.

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:07.760
Sites, materials, styles, scales...

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:09.760
They all collide.

00:42:08.920 --> 00:42:16.960
Buildings shout each other down in a cacophony of boastful machismo.

00:42:27.400 --> 00:42:32.280
I was going to say, it's anybody's guess if this remarkable skyline will endure.

00:42:31.960 --> 00:42:34.040
But it's too late for that.

00:42:33.440 --> 00:42:37.000
We know it won't.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:43.920
No city has the audacity to turn back the clock, especially when yesterday was so terrible.

00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:57.800
The last time I was on a Baltic ferry, 20 years ago, a courteous, smiling passenger handed my then six-year old twin daughters the contents of his wallet.

00:42:57.080 --> 00:43:06.760
And then walked away, showing a surprisingly good turn of speed and the exaggeratedly poised equilibrium of the very drunk.

00:43:07.040 --> 00:43:12.280
I felt obliged to return the money.

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:12.800
It took an age.

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:30.200
How do you distinguish one slumped blond, courteous, smiling oblivion-seeking giant with a blood alcohol level 20 times over the driving limit from the next slumped, blond courteous, smiling, oblivion-seeking...

00:43:30.560 --> 00:43:36.880
Oblivion-achieving, actually.

00:43:36.400 --> 00:43:43.600
They'd already achieved the northern goal of being somewhere else.

00:43:42.760 --> 00:43:44.880
In their head.

00:43:48.560 --> 00:44:03.240
Somewhere else, and some time else too, in a perpetual 1920s, in Recoleta in Buenos Aires, in a bordello where Carlos Gardel is singing forever.

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:08.520
Helsinki, home of the tango.

00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:10.400
Second home.

00:44:18.080 --> 00:44:26.280
The Finnish preoccupation with tango is a mystery unless you consider it an attempt at escape which has become naturalised.

00:44:25.520 --> 00:44:33.600
You escape from the forest to the city and you escape from the city to an illusory city.

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:49.480
Although Jorge Luis Borges, an occasional writer of tango lyrics, named one of his protagonists after Elias Lonnrot - more of whom later - Helsinki and Buenos Aires have little in common.

00:44:48.640 --> 00:45:08.160
Buenos Aires is devoted to psychoanalysis, la bella figura, the keeping up of appearances, sex and sobriety, without which the other four might run aground, what they do share is an almost fetishistic preoccupation with architecture and design.

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:12.480
In Helsinki, everyone knows who built what.

00:45:11.600 --> 00:45:19.560
Tram drivers, taxi drivers, taxidermists, fish-gutters, students, gamblers, waitresses.

00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:32.080
They all know for instance the name of the peripatetic German Carl Ludwig Engel, sometime city architect of Tallinn, who was brought to the attention of the Russian court.

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:41.000
He was commissioned to turn then colonial city of Helsinki into somewhere worthy of the Russian empire.

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:44.280
In other words, into a lesser St Petersburg.

00:45:43.320 --> 00:45:57.360
Engel worked in Helsinki through the second quarter of the 19th century and he built so prolifically that he turned a central area of the city into a homogenous whole.

00:45:57.240 --> 00:46:06.680
Stately, calm, sober, Imperial obviously, pompous, ordered, efficient and rather boring.

00:46:12.440 --> 00:46:19.640
There's none of the flashy charlatanry and theatrical genius of John Nash in London.

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:23.600
None of the gravity of Hamilton and Playfair in Edinburgh.

00:46:23.600 --> 00:46:28.600
None of the melancholy of Leo von Klenze at the Walhalla on the Danube.

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:35.600
And of course none of the horizontal bombast, let alone sheer size, of St Petersburg itself.

00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:52.560
Finns take inordinate pride, a small nation's pride, in the fact that the architect Alvar Aalto achieved such international recognition that, Sibelius apart, he is perhaps the best known Finnish artist of the 20th century.

00:46:53.640 --> 00:47:04.680
Aalto's reputation is less comprehensible to me than his compatriots' tango mania or their taste for eau de vie flavoured with tar.

00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:09.040
His architecture was the architecture of false modesty.

00:47:08.240 --> 00:47:14.400
His buildings shout about their sensitivity.

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:16.800
They boast how humane they are.

00:47:15.920 --> 00:47:18.040
They don't leave me cold.

00:47:17.320 --> 00:47:18.720
Far from it.

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:23.000
They rather infuriate me.

00:47:22.080 --> 00:47:41.000
With their coy, just-off-90-degree angles, their tweely audacious detailing, their insipid contrariness, their precocious undulations, their whimsical asymmetries, their precious tastefulness, their good manners that keep reminding you of their good manners.

00:47:40.200 --> 00:47:50.000
I am obviously missing something.

00:47:49.280 --> 00:47:52.800
For Aalto is not merely a national idol.

00:47:52.800 --> 00:47:58.600
An entire generation of British architects was in thrall to him.

00:47:57.800 --> 00:48:04.360
This was probably the last time that Britain was widely culturally beholden to the north.

00:48:07.440 --> 00:48:13.800
Though he had no direct involvement with it, the Festival of Britain had his fingerprints all over it.

00:48:13.080 --> 00:48:17.680
It was really the Festival of Aalto.

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:26.840
More than any other single architect, Aalto influenced the course of British design for over a decade.

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:40.080
His example was followed in numerous artlessly arty houses that architects and all purpose beardies built for themselves.

00:48:39.280 --> 00:48:48.680
Aalto is to Helsinki what Charles Rennie Mackintosh is to Glasgow.

00:48:48.320 --> 00:48:49.440
Industries have grown up around both.

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:53.400
More than cottage industries, substantial villa industries.

00:48:52.520 --> 00:49:01.680
Such commerce has caused their reputation to eclipse - to unjustly eclipse - that of artists who were their superior.

00:49:00.840 --> 00:49:09.640
Aalto's domestic renown today is at least partly due to the very fact of his having achieved international acclaim.

00:49:08.840 --> 00:49:21.280
In his lifetime it owed much his work being an explicit rejection of the self-consciously inward looking, specifically Finnish architects of the previous generation.

00:49:27.280 --> 00:49:34.120
That generation's inspiration had been predominantly literary.

00:49:33.240 --> 00:49:49.600
Its architecture is encrusted with allusions to the Kalevala, the folk saga compiled from oral sources, and partly invented, by Elias Lonnrot in the 1820s and 30s.

00:49:48.640 --> 00:49:53.400
The Kalevala was a spur to nationalism.

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:56.480
It's an inventory of...

00:49:55.720 --> 00:50:17.600
Incest, fratricide, revenge, berserkers, Virgin Birth, shamans, spells, giant pike, the land called Northland, hags, elks, fratricide, incest, reincarnation, Virgin Birth...

00:50:16.640 --> 00:50:25.120
By the turn of the 20th century, nationalism had become an international fashion.

00:50:24.840 --> 00:50:32.720
It was a widely prevalent mood to which the majority of Europe's smaller countries and aspirantly autonomous regions subscribed.

00:50:32.000 --> 00:50:38.680
They each attempted to create their own fittingly national architecture, an architecture that was specific to them.

00:50:37.920 --> 00:50:47.080
Catalonia, Liguria, Bohemia, Latvia, Georgia, Belgium, Lorraine.

00:50:46.120 --> 00:50:54.680
The problem was that these very different places tended to define themselves by kindred, sometimes identical means.

00:50:53.880 --> 00:50:59.600
They all adopted Art Nouveau... which perhaps defeated the point.

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:04.440
Finland took a different route.

00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:11.000
Finnish romantic nationalism was contemporary with Art Nouveau but owed little to it.

00:51:10.160 --> 00:51:19.040
Whatever precedents it has are to be found on the north-eastern seaboard of the United States and in Scotland.

00:51:18.280 --> 00:51:28.160
The master of this idiom was Lars Sonck, who seems never to have left his native country save to go to Sweden.

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:30.520
This may of course account for his comparative obscurity.

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:35.680
Sonck would eventually turn to a more conciliatory form of design.

00:51:35.640 --> 00:51:37.960
He got back on the straight and narrow.

00:51:37.680 --> 00:51:46.480
But as a young man he worked in a school of high and wild originality.

00:51:51.480 --> 00:52:06.520
The taste for back to basics - very basics - that Sonck and his contemporaries demonstrated goes far beyond that of H H Richardson in Boston or F T Pilkington in Edinburgh.

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:16.240
Between 1895 and 1905, a small group of Finnish architects created some of the most elemental buildings imaginable.

00:52:15.280 --> 00:52:18.840
Well, pseudo-elemental.

00:52:18.080 --> 00:52:24.120
For the devices are decorative rather than structural.

00:52:29.440 --> 00:52:37.280
They're calculated, sophisticated essays in appearing entirely unsophisticated.

00:52:36.560 --> 00:52:43.920
Highly accomplished works that try to look crude and unmade.

00:52:43.680 --> 00:52:45.680
They succeed.

00:52:47.000 --> 00:53:00.760
This is a form of architecture that borrows from geological formations just as music may incorporate or mimic the rush of the wind, birdsong or the gurgle of streams.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:04.680
It's a form of heightened naturalism.

00:53:03.760 --> 00:53:11.960
These buildings also refer to folk architecture just as contemporary music referred to folk songs.

00:53:11.120 --> 00:53:15.000
It's a form of ancestor worship.

00:53:14.120 --> 00:53:15.960
A very distant ancestor.

00:53:21.160 --> 00:53:30.560
It was hardly by chance that Jean Sibelius chose Sonck to design him a house.

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:35.080
Sibelius is the musical equivalent of Sonck.

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:50.680
Romantic nationalism came to be regarded as parochial, hick.

00:53:50.840 --> 00:53:58.160
And to many Finns today, dogged devotees of modernism, it still seems faintly embarrassing.

00:53:57.480 --> 00:54:03.000
To this non-Finn however it seems magnificent.

00:54:02.160 --> 00:54:04.920
Romantic nationalism was necessary.

00:54:04.160 --> 00:54:07.680
It wasn't whimsical.

00:54:06.720 --> 00:54:08.280
It wasn't fanciful.

00:54:07.520 --> 00:54:15.080
It had a purpose.

00:54:18.680 --> 00:54:35.600
Its formal exaggerations, its groundedness, its shunning of elegance, its rough and ready butchness may seem like Village People turned to stone, but there was an earnest purpose.

00:54:34.920 --> 00:54:54.920
This was architecture as civil disobedience, aesthetics in the service of collective identity and political agitation in the face of Russian colonialism.

00:55:20.640 --> 00:55:37.800
Before it was used in an architectural context to describe the differently textured and incised surfaces that relieve a building's predominant material, the word rustication meant "returning to the country".

00:55:51.920 --> 00:55:52.960
To rusticate an undergraduate for a misdemeanour meant to send him to the country.

00:56:05.600 --> 00:56:11.880
This massive, rocky rustication suggests more than merely returning to the country.

00:56:10.960 --> 00:56:24.720
It represents a north that does not escape from itself by faking the south, by deluding itself about its climate, by making believe that it's somewhere that it's not.

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:48.800
It is an architecture of realism, founded in a pragmatic response to the place where it is built.

00:56:48.960 --> 00:56:53.240
Which is not to say that it shuns escapism.

00:56:52.320 --> 00:56:56.160
But its escapism is of a contrary kind.

00:56:55.200 --> 00:56:59.880
The flight it implies has no wings but feet weighted with stones.

00:57:00.040 --> 00:57:06.840
It offers the heavy solaces of the set, the warren, the dark warmth of hibernation.

00:57:06.320 --> 00:57:14.000
This was the architecture of a nation seeking a different state.

00:57:14.040 --> 00:57:19.520
The doorways of these buildings are tiny apertures in cliffs of rough stone.

00:57:18.680 --> 00:57:21.600
This surely is the proper response to the benighted north.

00:57:21.040 --> 00:57:29.880
To burrow into the loam and peat we came from, to drink from the mother and satisfy our uterine longing.

00:57:29.680 --> 00:57:32.360
Womb, tomb...

00:57:31.560 --> 00:57:34.240
There's just a letter's difference.

00:57:33.480 --> 00:57:37.800
Who's to say which is the darker.
